{# THE BOTTOM RUNG — the document.

   The four standard block names, unchanged, because a module author who knows
   Twig already knows them and renaming them to shell_* would have bought
   consistency with confusion.

   A page may extend THIS rung directly: a print view, a PDF export, an embedded
   panel. It still carries the theme, because a printed page with no tokens is
   black Times New Roman on white and does not look like the product it came
   from. #}
<!DOCTYPE html>
{# `fold` on <html>, the one element every page has: it eases every fold on
   the page — the sidebar, the page index, any table row whose hidden changes —
   so it sits outside anything a page draws (the sheet's "HOUSE — THE FOLD"). #}
<html lang="{{ shell_lang|default('en') }}" data-controller="uhifadhi--shell-bundle--fold">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">

    {# The page says what it is; the shell says where it is and whose it is. #}
    {%- set _page %}{% block title %}{% endblock %}{% endset -%}
    <title>{{ shell_title(_page) }}</title>

    {# THE TAB ICON. Declared here rather than left to the host, because a
       document that declares none is a document every browser asks
       `/favicon.ico` for — and a fresh installation answered that with a 404 on its
       first minute. It is the full masterbrand mark as a single SVG: one file
       at every size, both palettes inside it. A deployment with a brand of its
       own overrides `stylesheets` and links its own after this one. #}
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="{{ asset('bundles/shell/favicon.svg') }}">

    {# THE THEME, BEFORE THE FIRST PAINT — not by a controller that connects
       after the first frame. A visitor who chose dark must never be shown a
       white page first, and "system" is a real third answer rather than a
       synonym for light: somebody who told their operating system which they
       want has already answered.

       This is inline, in the head, ABOVE the stylesheet, on purpose. It is the
       one script in the shell, it is four lines, and every one of them is load-
       bearing. #}
    <script>
        (function () {
            try {
                // Four answers, in the order they outrank each other: what this
                // link asked for, what the visitor chose, what the deployment
                // defaults to, and — if that default is "system" — what the
                // operating system prefers. The link override is here rather
                // than in a controller so that a screenshot or a projector gets
                // the palette it asked for on the FIRST frame.
                var asked = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search).get('theme');
                var choice = asked || localStorage.getItem('shell-theme')
                    || '{{ shell_default_theme()|e('js') }}';
                var dark = choice === 'dark' || (choice === 'system'
                    && window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches);
                document.documentElement.classList.toggle('dark', dark);

                // THE SIDEBAR'S WIDTH IS THE SAME KIND OF FACT, and it is
                // resolved here for the same reason: a remembered rail applied
                // when the sidebar's controller connects is a 236px sidebar
                // that jumps to 66px after the first frame, on every load.
                // <html> is the only element that exists this early, so the
                // stylesheet draws the rail from a class on it as well.
                document.documentElement.classList.toggle('shell-rail',
                    localStorage.getItem('shell-sidebar') === 'rail');
            } catch (e) { /* a browser that refuses to remember still gets a themed page */ }
        })();
    </script>

    {% block stylesheets %}
        {# The shell's sheet, first. A module base calls parent() and then adds
           its own, because its rules are written to override these. #}
        <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset('bundles/shell/shell.css') }}">

        {# THEN THE SHEETS THE COMPONENTS NEED, before any page's own.
           A component — an atlas month, an atlas chart — is written into a
           page whose package has never heard of it, so the page cannot link a
           sheet for it and the component cannot link one for itself: a
           stylesheet link outside the head is not conforming HTML. The head
           asks instead. See Contract\StylesheetSourceInterface. #}
        {% for sheet in shell_stylesheets() %}
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset(sheet) }}">
        {% endfor %}
    {% endblock %}

    {% block javascripts %}
        {# THE IMPORTMAP, RENDERED BY DEFAULT — and this is a change of mind the
           bug report earned. The socket was declared empty on the reasoning
           that a bundle cannot contribute an importmap entry. True, and beside
           the point: a shell whose own controls need Stimulus and whose own
           document never emits the importmap ships furniture that cannot work,
           which is precisely what a fresh installation had. The shell requires
           symfony/asset-mapper and symfony/stimulus-bundle, so `app` is the
           entrypoint every installation of it has.

           A host with a different entrypoint fills this socket with its own
           line; the socket is unchanged and so is its name. #}
        {% block importmap %}{{ importmap('app') }}{% endblock %}
    {% endblock %}
</head>
{# `shell_body_attributes` — the one variable, rather than block, in the whole
   contract, and it exists because attributes on <body> are not content: a block
   cannot put them there. A host or a module sets it at the top of a template,
   outside any block, and it reaches this tag:

     {% set shell_body_attributes = map_basemap_attributes() %}

   That is how a platform-wide setting every map controller reads — the one
   place a deployment's basemap is decided — rides on a document the shell owns
   without the shell knowing that maps exist.

   IT IS EMITTED AS MARKUP, because attributes are markup and an escaped
   `data-x="y"` is a broken tag. Whatever a host puts here is its own to escape:
   build it from values you control, never from a request. #}
{# `localtime` on the <body> the shell owns, not on any element a module draws:
   the frame localises every `<time datetime>` on the page to the reader's own
   zone, so a module names no controller and its templates stay portable to a
   host that renders through this same frame. `shell_body_attributes` never
   carries a second `data-controller` (it is basemap data only), so the two do
   not collide. See docs/theming.md, "A time reads in the reader's zone".

   `confirm-modal` rides here for a different reason: a module's destructive
   button names the controller as `confirm-modal`, not as this bundle's
   composer-derived identifier, because a module must not have to spell the
   shell's package name to ask a question. Mounted once on the body, the
   controller registers itself under that short public name, and every
   trigger in every module binds to it. The body instance has no trigger and
   asks nothing. See docs/theming.md, "One question before something is
   destroyed". #}
<body data-controller="uhifadhi--shell-bundle--localtime uhifadhi--shell-bundle--confirm-modal"{% if shell_body_attributes is defined %} {{ shell_body_attributes|raw }}{% endif %}>
{% block body %}{% endblock %}
</body>
</html>
